{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_123","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting!\n\nPreliminary results show isotonic regression alone performs worse than bias correction, and blending a small amount of isotonic with bias correction brings a minor MAE improvement. The current test MAE is anomalously low due to a favorable train-test split, and a regularization optimization for isotonic regression is planned as the next attempt.\n\nAfter exploring several feasible improvement approaches, I've settled on a global ridge regression model incorporating one-hot features for nodes and horizons, plus base, anchor, history, and clock inputs. Appropriate regularization is expected to support good generalization, and the available dataset has sufficient volume for a moderately complex model.\n\nI've finalized the feature set for the global ridge regression model, which includes main effects of all inputs, node and horizon indicators paired with ridge penalty. I'll first implement the simplified version with per-node intercept, conduct proper cross-validation, and then evaluate the model."}}
